> **Models a machine has disabled.** `hermes-comfy.conf` can list models this machine must not use in
> `IMGGEN_DISABLED_MODELS` (for example a model whose licence does not cover the occasion);
> `gen.py` refuses them. Check `"error"` and choose another model from the table below.
> - With the `qwen21` family disabled, use `flux` (English prompt) for text-to-image, and
>   `img2img --image <path>` to redraw an image the user gave.
> - No other model takes `--ref`. For a picture of a **reference character** (an image the user gave),
>   make it a frame of a short video: vidgen `r2v "<scene, full body, facing the camera, she does not
>   speak, static camera>" --ref <character image> --ref <location image> --short 480 --seconds 3
>   --wait`, then take a frame 80% in: `ffmpeg -v error -y -ss 2.4 -i <clip.mp4> -frames:v 1 <out.png>`.
>   One H3 segment holds the references; at 480p that covers 3 s, while a 720p segment is only
>   1.62 s, too short for the location to appear. Tell the user the picture is a still from a clip.


# imggen — local image generation
**Always reply to the user in Traditional Chinese (zh-Hant).** These instructions are in English; the user speaks Chinese.

When generating images, **use only this skill's `gen.py`**. It automatically starts a memory-protected ComfyUI,
applies a verified workflow, and returns the output file paths.

## Iron rules (must follow)

**The only legal image-generation action is running the line below.** Check it yourself before sending: if the command string does not
contain the text `imggen/gen.py`, it is wrong — stop and use this instead:
```
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" <model> "<prompt>"
```

**Copy the line above exactly; do not "helpfully" rewrite the paths.** The absolute paths of the interpreter and `gen.py` were filled in at install time, and they
always use **forward slashes** (Python on Windows accepts forward slashes too). Two pitfalls hit in real tests:

- Hermes's terminal on Windows is bash, so the backslashes in `C:\Users\...` get eaten and it becomes `C:Users<you>...`.
- A bare `python` on Windows hits the Microsoft Store stub (`Python was not found`); on Linux it may
  point to the system python instead of the one that has the dependencies. That is why the line above **uses an absolute path even for the interpreter**.

If you hit one of these, do not invent another workaround; just change the command to the form above and rerun it.

**Run that line with the `terminal` tool; do not wrap it in `execute_code`.** Calling `gen.py` with `subprocess.run(...)` inside
`execute_code` also counts as a detour: that sandbox is killed outright after 300 seconds, so `gen.py` gets killed before it can tell you
"still rendering", and you get nothing back. Measured: because of this the model resubmitted four times with four different models, all four images queued up behind each other in
ComfyUI, and not a single one reached the user.

Everything below counts as a detour and is forbidden without exception — **every one of these has failed in real tests** (hand-written scripts crashed on their own, images got generated but nobody knew where they went,
bypassing memory protection got the LLM resident on the same machine squeezed out):
- Writing your own `.py`/`.json` file and running it (`gen_xxx.py`, `run_workflow.py` and the like). `run_workflow.py` **does not exist** on this machine; do not go looking for it.
- Using `execute_code` to build a workflow dict in memory and submit it
- Using `urllib`/`requests` to call `/prompt` or `/history` on `127.0.0.1:8188`
- Running `comfy launch` yourself, filling in `filename_prefix` yourself, or picking checkpoint/VAE/CLIP file names yourself
- Using `computer_use` to click around ComfyUI's web interface
- Using PIL/numpy/OpenCV to cut out, paste and layer images yourself to "composite" characters and a background (measured: red hair was removed as background, people floated in mid-air, and hand gestures could not be changed at all). Compositing, background swaps and putting several characters in one frame always use `qwen21_edit`, with the background as the first `--ref`
- Reading "fast mode" as something else: the `qwen21` family **is turbo (fast) by default**; add `--no-turbo` only when the user asks for "highest quality / take your time"

**When `gen.py` fails, detours are even more forbidden.** The correct response has only three steps: read the `error` it prints → adjust using the parameters written in this document
(change `<model>`, rewrite the prompt, add `--seed`) → run the same `gen.py` again. If it keeps failing, report the `error` text verbatim to
the user and stop; **inventing your own path only makes it slower and more wrong**.

**Always report when it finishes**: paste the absolute paths from `outputs` to the user. Generated but not reported = not done.

## How to use
**Always use the full absolute path of `gen.py`** (it uses absolute paths internally and runs from any directory; do not use a relative path, do not cd first, do not assume it is in your working directory):
```
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" <model> "<prompt>" [--image <path>] [--ref <path> ...] [--denoise <0-1>] [--seed <int>] [--no-turbo] [--move-x <fraction>] [--move-y <fraction>] [--keep-loaded]
```

`--ref` can be repeated (up to 16 images) and is **supported only by `qwen21` / `qwen21_edit`** (`qwen21_move` takes only 1 image): each reference image is fed to the Qwen3-VL vision tower so its content is understood, and its VAE latent is attached to the sequence.
In the prompt, use `<image1>`, `<image2>`… to refer to the Nth `--ref` (same order as on the command line).

- `qwen21 --ref`: **generates a new image**; the character/style stays close to the reference, but composition, scene and pose are redone according to the prompt. Good for "this character standing on a Tokyo street".
- `qwen21_edit --ref`: **edits an image**; the first `--ref` (`<image1>`) is the image being modified, and the output keeps its size and composition, changing only what the prompt asks for.
  Good for "change the outfit / change the background / add something / keep everything else". The other `--ref` images are materials (for example `<image2>` is the clothing to put on).

### How to write `qwen21_edit` prompts (follows the official ComfyUI template, verified in tests)
Use **a single English instruction sentence** that states explicitly "what to change" plus "what to keep":
```
Keep the character, face, hair and pose in <image1> unchanged, replace the outfit with a white hoodie and black joggers, keep the original framing, background and lighting.
```
- **Always list what to keep** (face / hair / pose / background / framing). If you only write "Replace the outfit", the model freely rebuilds the pose.
- **Do not ask for things outside the frame**: the original image only goes down to the thighs, but you ask for "white sneakers" → the model turns the person into a crouching/sitting pose to squeeze the shoes into the frame.
- **Do not describe the character's appearance at length in the prompt**: the appearance comes from `<image1>`; a text description overrides the reference image and makes the character drift.
- `--image <image>` also works; it is equivalent to the first `--ref`.
- **Moving a person's position (shift left / right / up / down)**: `qwen21_edit` cannot do it (three tests, nothing moved); **use `qwen21_move` instead**, see "Moving position" below.

Eight models:
| model | What it is | When to pick it |
|-------|------|--------|
| `qwen21`  | Qwen-Image-2.1 (20B, int8 + Qwen3-VL-8B encoder), **new generation, highest prompt adherence, can draw correct Chinese characters** | **Default first choice**: complex prompt / must follow precisely, Chinese subject matter, text inside the image. Beats the old `qwen` in quality and adherence |
| `qwen21_edit` | Qwen-Image-2.1 image editing (same model, the reference image is the edit target; follows the official template: no PE, cfg 1) | An image was given and should be **edited locally with everything else kept**: change outfit, change background, add/remove objects, change hand gesture. **`--ref` is required**. Turbo by default: about 36 s with 1 ref, about 48 s with 2 (90 / 118 s with `--no-turbo`) |
| `qwen21_move` | **Shifts** the person/subject across the whole image: crops one side, hands the strip freed on the other side to Qwen-Image-2.1 to extend the background, and **pastes all other pixels back untouched** | The user wants to "move left/right/up/down" or "leave more space on the right". Requires `--ref` + `--move-x`/`--move-y`. About 36 s |
| turbo (default) | `qwen21` / `qwen21_edit` / `qwen21_move` **use the Viggle Turbo LoRA v0.2.1 by default** (6 steps, no CFG, LoRA not merged) | Measured (RTX 5060 Ti): text-to-image 70→22 s, edit with 1 ref 90→36 s, with 2 refs 118→48 s, 3 refs also fine, no visible quality difference. If the image needs **small text**, or a multi-image composite shows **ghosting / duplicated people**, retry with `--no-turbo` (base, 25 steps); passing `--steps` also switches to base automatically; if this ComfyUI lacks the turbo nodes or the LoRA it **falls back to base automatically**, and the `turbo` field of the output JSON reads `off (base sampler): <reason>` — just tell the user plainly "this time it used normal speed" |
| `qwen`    | Qwen-Image (20B, Qwen2.5-VL encoder), high prompt adherence, can draw correct Chinese characters | Old-version fallback; the backup when the `qwen21` model files are missing |
| `flux`    | FLUX.1-schnell (12B GGUF Q8, Apache license), 4 steps, strong aesthetics and detail, good prompt adherence | Want the Flux look / Western aesthetics, English prompt, **commercial use** (clean license). Medium speed (4 steps + weight offload) |
| `zimage`  | Z-Image Turbo, 8 steps, fast | Want speed, cartoon/illustration/simple subjects |
| `realvis` | RealVisXL, photorealistic | Want real portraits / real scenes / product shots (fast) |
| `img2img` | Generates a new image from a provided image | The user gave an image and wants its style changed / it redrawn |

### Moving position: `qwen21_move`
```
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" qwen21_move "" --ref "<previous turn's output>" --move-x -0.15
```
- `--move-x`: a fraction of the width, **negative moves left, positive moves right**; `--move-y`: a fraction of the height, **negative moves up, positive moves down**. Both can be given together. Maximum 0.4.
- "Move left a little" ≈ `-0.1`–`-0.15`; "move left a lot" ≈ `-0.25`; "leave some more space on the right" = move left.
- The prompt can be left empty `""`; to say what should be drawn in the newly freed area, write it in the prompt (e.g. `"more palm trees"`).
- The person and the original background are **pixel-for-pixel unchanged** (verified in tests); only the new strip is drawn by the AI, so nothing drifts.
- **Limitation**: moving left crops off the leftmost strip of the original image. If the person is already touching that side, the body gets cut off → look at the image first; the move must be smaller than the distance from the person to that edge; report the result truthfully.
- The same applies in multi-turn conversations: use the previous turn's output as `--ref`.
- **When reporting the move, copy `move.moved` from the JSON verbatim** (e.g. `left 160 px (15% of the width)`); do not estimate it by eye. In a test an agent once described 15% as "leaving 1/3 more space".

## ⚠️ Check what language the prompt is in before choosing a model (the step most likely to fail silently)

**`realvis` and `flux` cannot read Chinese/Japanese/Korean.** Their text encoders are SDXL's CLIP and
T5-XXL+CLIP-L respectively, and they only understand English. A Chinese prompt sent to them **does not raise an error**; instead it produces an image that is "clean-looking,
but completely unrelated to what you asked for" — this kind of failure is the hardest to notice, because the JSON comes back `ok`.

Real test (the same Chinese prompt「一隻橘色小貓坐在木頭窗台上曬太陽,寫實攝影風格」— an orange kitten sunbathing on a wooden windowsill, realistic photo style):

| model | Encoder | Chinese | Test result |
|---|---|---|---|
| `qwen21` | Qwen3-VL-8B | ✅ | Correct (new-generation encoder) |
| `qwen` | Qwen2.5-VL-7B | ✅ | Correct |
| `zimage` / `img2img` | Qwen3-4B | ✅ | Correct |
| `realvis` | SDXL CLIP | ❌ | Produced **a woman in a bathrobe and a dog on a bed** |
| `flux` | T5-XXL + CLIP-L | ❌ | Produced **a night street scene + garbled calligraphy** |

**So the rule is**: when the user describes the image in Chinese —

- Wants the `realvis`/`flux` style → **translate the prompt into English first**, then send it (translate it yourself; do not send it as-is)
- Does not care which model → use `qwen21` or `zimage` directly; they understand Chinese natively

`gen.py` blocks the "Chinese prompt + realvis/flux" combination and suggests the two routes above. **When you see that error, do what it says**;
do not rephrase and force it through.

**License**: the `qwen21` family (Qwen-Image-2.1) and Viggle Turbo are under the **Qwen research license, non-commercial use only**. When the user says it is for **commercial use** (ads, product images, things to be sold), use `flux` or the old `qwen` (Apache, commercial use allowed) instead, and tell them why.

**Model selection key points**: the user complains「跟 prompt 不像/沒照做」(doesn't match the prompt / didn't do what I said) → use `qwen21` (or the old `qwen`). Want Flux aesthetics / an **English** prompt / a clean commercial license → `flux`. Want speed, simple subject → `zimage`; want a realistic photo → `realvis` (remember to write the prompt in English).
`qwen21` is the new generation of Qwen-Image (2.1); it beats the old `qwen` in quality and adherence, so prefer it for complex/Chinese prompts; `flux` renders Chinese text worse than the qwen family, but its overall aesthetics and detail are outstanding.

On success it prints `{"status":"ok","outputs":["<absolute path>"]}`; on failure it prints `{"status":"error",...}`.
Report the output paths to the user.

### When it prints `"status":"running"` (slow card, large model)

`gen.py` waits in the foreground for at most about 7 minutes; if the image is not finished by then, it prints:

```
{"status":"running","prompt_id":"<id>","progress":"step 6/8","next":"<full command to fetch the result>"}
```

This is **not a failure**. ComfyUI finishes rendering in the background on its own, and the image will not be lost. In this case:

1. **Do not resubmit the same image** — resubmitting only queues another identical one behind it, which is slower. (`gen.py` also guards against this: if the same image is still queued, it simply takes over that one.)
2. Tell the user "still rendering, currently at `progress`", and give the `prompt_id`.
3. To get the result, run the `next` line (i.e. the line below). If it is not done yet it prints `running` again; once done it prints `ok` and the image path:
   ```
   "@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" status <prompt_id>
   "@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" status <prompt_id> --wait 300     # wait up to another 300 seconds
   ```

Only `"status":"error"` is a failure, for example "made no progress for 600 s" (ComfyUI is stuck)
or "not in ComfyUI's queue or history" (the server was restarted; render it again).

## Examples (note: all use the absolute path of gen.py)
```
# text-to-image (default zimage)
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" zimage "一隻戴太空頭盔的柴犬,扁平向量插畫,粗黑外框"

# realistic photo
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" realvis "rainy Taipei street at night, neon reflections, 35mm"

# image-to-image: change the style but keep the composition (<absolute path of the user's image> = absolute path of the image the user gave)
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" img2img "glossy 3D rendered toy, soft studio light, white background" --image "<absolute path of the user's image>" --denoise 0.85

# qwen21 + reference image: same character, new scene (--ref can be repeated, up to 16 images)
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" qwen21 "The character from <image1> standing on a rainy Tokyo street at night, neon reflections, same anime illustration style" --ref "<reference image>"

# qwen21_edit: edit the image, change only the outfit, keep the rest
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" qwen21_edit "Keep the character, face, hair and pose in <image1> unchanged, replace the outfit with a white hoodie and black joggers, keep the original framing, background and lighting." --ref "<image to edit>"

# qwen21_edit: put the clothing from <image2> on the character in <image1>
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" qwen21_edit "Keep the character and pose in <image1> unchanged, put the shirt from <image2> on the character, keep the original background and lighting." --ref "<character image>" --ref "<clothing image>"

# qwen21_edit: one background + several characters (background goes first; the output size follows the background)
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" qwen21_edit "Place the character from <image2> on the left and the character from <image3> on the right of the scene in <image1>, keep both characters' faces, hair and outfits unchanged, keep the background unchanged, match the lighting." --ref "<background image>" --ref "<character A>" --ref "<character B>"

# multi-turn refinement: each next turn always uses "the previous turn's output" as the first --ref; the prompt only states this turn's new change + the keep list
"@@PY@@" "@@IMGGEN@@" qwen21_edit "Keep the character, face, hair, outfit and background in <image1> unchanged, change her right hand to a peace sign, keep the original framing." --ref "<previous turn's output>"
```

## Why you must go through `gen.py` (memory protection, same scheme as vidgen)

Image generation and video generation use **the same ComfyUI**, so the same protection rule applies fully here —
image generation also loads models of several GB and will likewise squeeze the LLM resident on the same machine.

**The key to the protection is `--reserve-vram`, and it is decided "at the moment ComfyUI starts"; it cannot be changed afterwards.**
`start_comfy.py` first measures the VRAM **already occupied by others** (the resident LLM is in there), then uses that to
decide how much to reserve for it, so that ComfyUI does not treat that memory as space it can use freely.
`gen.py` ensures this automatically: if ComfyUI is not running it starts it the protected way; if it is already running it reuses it.

So the following two things directly break the protection:

1. **Running `comfy launch` / starting ComfyUI yourself** — no reserve, and the LLM can get squeezed out at any moment.
2. **Bypassing `gen.py` and calling the API directly** — you connect to a server that may or may not be protected.

**If ComfyUI was started by someone else (or by you in a previous turn) without a reserve**, `gen.py` prints a warning on stderr
and tells you how to restart it. When you see that warning, do what it says; do not ignore it — it means this render risks squeezing out the LLM.

> **The measurement is GPU-vendor neutral**: the engine only asks "how much of this card is already occupied, and how much is left".
> **Vendor tools first; only if none are available does it ask a service API**: `VIDGEN_GPU_QUERY_CMD` (custom) → `nvidia-smi`
> → `intel-ze` (Intel Level Zero) → `xpu-smi` (Intel CLI) → `rocm-smi` (AMD)
> → ComfyUI's `/system_stats` → TRUSTA-AST's `/system/resources`.
> Non-NVIDIA accelerators go down the same path; you do not need to change how you use it.

## Rules
1. **Translation is your job, and you must do it proactively, not wait to be blocked.**
   The user describes the image in Chinese/Japanese/Korean and you picked `realvis` or `flux` → **translate it into English yourself before sending**.
   You are a bilingual model, and you hold the user's full intent, so your translation will fit better than any automatic translation.
   `gen.py`'s block is a safety net, not part of the workflow — hitting it means you skipped this step.
   (`qwen21`/`qwen`/`zimage`/`img2img` understand Chinese natively; no translation needed.)
2. **Do not compute a seed yourself**; it is random by default. Pass `--seed <number>` (a number) only when you need to reproduce a result.
3. **img2img denoise**: 0.6 ≈ almost unchanged, **0.85 = change the style and keep the composition (default starting point)**, 0.95 ≈ nearly a full redraw.
4. The user only says「生一張…」(make a…) without specifying a style → use `zimage` (fast). Wants **high quality / a complex description / Chinese** → `qwen21`. Says「照片/寫實」(photo/realistic) → `realvis`. Gave an image and wants its **style changed / it redrawn** → `img2img`; gave an image and wants a **new image that keeps the character/style/subject** → `qwen21 --ref <image>` (several allowed); gave an image and wants **only part of it changed (outfit/background), everything else kept as is** → `qwen21_edit --ref <image>`.
5. To swap the underlying model, edit the workflow files in `@@ENGINE@@/workflows/`, not here.
   Those are **for humans to edit**; when you generate an image, do not read or edit those files — just run `gen.py`.

6. **After generating, VRAM is handed back by default (`"vram": "freed"`)**; you do not need to do anything.
   Add `--keep-loaded` only when generating several images in a row, to save the model reload for each one;
   but it keeps the GPU occupied the whole time — measured: a resident image model eats 3.1 GB,
   and `vidgen plan`'s available budget drops from 7.4 GB to 5.6 GB, making it recommend an unnecessarily downsized video.
   So **after the burst is finished, run once more without the flag, or POST `/free` yourself**, and only then go plan a video.
   The co-resident LLM is also waiting for this VRAM.
## Where it runs
`gen.py` references the workflows and the launcher by absolute path, so it can be run from any directory. Output goes by default to
`@@OUT@@`; override with `--out`.
